\layout Subsection

Characteristics of the Systems
\layout Description

Circuit\SpecialChar ~
Switching is wasteful in terms of bandwidth, as the communicating
 nodes may not be utilizing all the available bandwidth reserved for them
 all of the time.
 However, once connection is established, a guaranteed channel is provided
 with a known communication delay associated with it.
 Note that the sum of rates for all sessions using a link cannot exceed
 the total capacity of the link.
 New sessions must be rejected when the link capacity is reached.
 The approach is used for telephone networks where the session rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{s}$
\end_inset 

, is the same for each session.
 Switching would be more complex for data networks and the nature of transmissio
n may also mean that the links are efficiently utilized 
\layout Description

Store-and-Forward\SpecialChar ~
Switching makes efficient use of bandwidth because whilst
 communication between two nodes may be idle for a period of time, packets
 from another communication may be sent along the same line.
\layout Standard

Alice and Bob both have telephone lines.
 Alice decides to call Bob using the phone line.
 Here the make use of a circuit switching network provided by their telephone
 supplier.
 The session dominates both phone lines preventing use by other `clients'.
